{"agent":"fleet-dedupenamed","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The `or rows_` fallback silently defeats the exact separation this function exists to provide. `_only_named(r, floor)` is true precisely for a candidate that is tagged `[named]`, not `identical`, and scores under the floor — i.e. the records from bug Bf29855c414. When *every* row is such a record, the comprehension on line 11 evaluates to `[]`, the `or rows_` makes `asking` the whole list, and therefore `named = [r for r in rows_ if r not in asking]` on line 12 is empty. Given the motivating input `[{\"id\":\"P-acp\", ..., \"score\":0.2, \"flags\":[\"named\"]}, {\"id\":\"P-spawn\", ..., \"score\":0.15, \"flags\":[\"named\"]}]` with `floor=0.5`, the function emits `refused: possible duplicate. It reads like:` followed by `P-acp` and `P-spawn` as the cause of the refusal, and the `Also named by the text (information only, not why this was refused):` section is never produced. That is the same assertion the fix is supposed to end (\"A phase whose body says 'reuses P-acp, P-spawn' is not a duplicate of them\"), and it directly contradicts the docstring promise that a record listed only because the text names its id \"is shown apart, as information: it is not what makes the add refuse.\" The fallback should not be there (the caller must guarantee a non-`only_named` candidate, otherwise the add must not be refused at all); as written, any caller that hands this function an all-named candidate set gets a message that misattributes the refusal to the named ids.","digest":"9f1e9fcb4edc3405","location":"ddflow/api/_dedupe_reason.py:11 (`reason`, the `asking = ... or rows_` line)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/_dedupe_reason.py:11 (`reason`, the `asking = ... or rows_` line)"}],"chunks_total":3,"coverage":"3/3 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":10327,"diff_sha":"e1a5919ca42aac6bb84546abf793449bbac053e133b0ef7a7bfd68ec8d3d42c7","